1.Nf3 {Notes by Nimzowitsch An instructive example of the
method of dealing with the supports of a blockader. In the
endgame they are usually driven away, in the middlegame on the
other hand - kept busy.} e6 2.d4 d5 3.e3 Nf6 4.b3 Nbd7 {He
should have played ...c5 and ...Nc6.} 5.Bd3 c6 6.O-O Bd6 7.Bb2
Qc7 {In order to play ...e5 and thus open up the game. To
prevent this, White undertakes a counter attack.} 8.c4 b6 {If
now 8...e5, the continuation would be 9 c5 Be7 (9...e4 10 exd6
Qxd6 11 Ba3 etc.) 10 dxe5 Ng4 11 b4 Ngxe5 12 Nxe5 Nxe5 13 f4
Nxd3 14 Qxd3 and White commands the diagonal b2 to g7.} 9.Nc3
Bb7 10.Rc1 Rc8 11.cxd5 exd5 12.e4 {White opens all the lines.}
dxe4 13.Nxe4 Nxe4 14.Bxe4 O-O 15. d5 c5 {The two bishops have
now a clear line of fire to the enemy king's wing. Impressed
by this the second player is inclined to underestimate the
fact that the d-pawn is now passed; in fact to overlook it
altogether. And indeed what possible road could this most
carefully blockaded passed pawn play? Why, a reserve blockader
is already atationed at d7! But things turn out quite
otherwise.} 16.Re1 Qd8 17.Bb1 {This attack leads to the
instructive results that the blockading pieces, the bishop at
d6 and knight at d7 are either cut off or killed off.} Re8
18.Qd3 {First Rxe8+ would have been more precise.} Nf8
{...Rxe1+ would have been better.} 19.Rxe8 Qxe8 20.Nh4 {!} f6
21.Nf5 Rd8 {Black is about to try to show up the weakness of
White's d-pawn, when he is wakened from his dream by the flash
of a sacrifice.} 22.Bxf6 {!} Bxh2+ {! In order not to lose a
pawn Black must submit to this indirect exchange of his
bishop. If 22...gxf6 then 23 Nxd6 Rxd6 24 Qg3+} 23.Kxh2 gxf6
{What a change! the bishop at d6 has disappeared and the
reserve blockader the land at d7 will soon land at g6. The
d-pawn is therefore free!} 24.Qg3+ Ng6 25.f4 {To allow Re1;
the passed pawn is indirectly protected.} Kh8 {Not 25...B or
Rxd5 because of 26 Re1 and 27 Ne7+ etc.} 26.Re1 Qf8 {! If
26...Qg8, the passed pawn would have come into his own in a
most interesting manner; namely as follows : 27 Ne7 Nxe7 28
Rxe7 (7th rank) Qxg3+ 29 Kxg3 Rg8+ 30 Kf2 Rg7. Apparently the
7th rank is now neutralized, but the passed pawn has something
to say to that. 32 d6 Rxe7 32 dxe7 Bc6 33 Be4 Be8 34 f5! Kg7
35 Bd5 and now the pawn at e7 is unassailable. 35... Kh6 36
Kf3 Kg5 37 Ke4 and Black is powerless against the threat Bb7,
Kd5, and Bc6, and the blockader must die.} 27.d6 {!} Rd7 {Why
not ...Bc8, would not this have led to the winning of the
passed pawn? The answer is no, for the continuation would be
28 Ne7 (By his p-d6 White has provided an outpost atation at
e7) 28...Qh6+ (best) 29 Kg1 Nxf4 and now 30 Nxc8 Rxc8 31 d7
and wins.} 28. Qc3 {Threatening 29 Re8! Qxe8 30 Qxf6+ Kg8 31
Nh6 mate. Accordingly the 8th rank must be safeguarded by the
retreat ...Rd8. But in this case the 7th rank will be left
without protection and White wins by Re7. Note that the
winning moves Re7 or Ne7 (as in the last note), must be
regarded as a direct consequence of the passed pawn's
advance.} Rxd6 {A desperate expedient. If 28...Rf7 then 29 d7
Rxd7 30 Re8! would have been immediately decisive.} 29.Nxd6
Qxd6 30.Bxg6 hxg6 31.Re8+ Kg7 32.Qg3 Bc6 33.Re3 Bd7 34.f5 {!}
Qxg3+ 35.Kxg3 Bxf5 36.Re7+ Kh6 37.Rxa7 Bb1 38.Ra6 b5 39.a4
bxa4 40.bxa4 Kg5 41.Rb6 Be4 42.a5 f5 43.a6 c4 44.a7 c3 45.Rb3
f4+ 46.Kf2 c2 47.Rc3 1-0